+# Review Draft
+
+## Context
+
+A clean `node:24-bookworm` linux/amd64 container could install `ai-wiki-toolkit@0.1.7`, but running `aiwiki-toolkit --version` failed with a Python shared library or GLIBC runtime compatibility error.
+
+The failing binary was downstream of the GitHub Release artifact and then repackaged into the Linux npm platform package.
+
+## What Went Wrong
+
+The release flow verified that the package could build and publish, but it did not execute the Linux artifact in the oldest supported downstream runtime before publishing.
+
+Building Linux binaries on a newer runner can silently raise the glibc baseline. That class of failure can be invisible until a user runs the packaged binary in a distro image with an older glibc.
+
+## Fix
+
+Keep the Linux build baseline intentionally older than the downstream smoke target, then execute the artifact in the downstream target before any package publication.
+
+For this repo, the practical release guard is:
+
+- build `linux-x64` on `ubuntu-22.04`
+- run the archived binary in `node:24-bookworm` before uploading release artifacts
+- pack the Linux npm package plus meta package and run the global wrapper in `node:24-bookworm` before `npm publish`
+
+## Reuse Assessment
+
+This is reusable for PyInstaller-backed package-manager wrappers.
+
+When a release artifact is consumed by npm, Homebrew, or another package layer, CI should smoke-test the final packaged runtime path in a representative target container instead of relying only on build-host execution.
+
+## Promotion Decision
+
+Keep as a draft for now. Promote if another Linux binary compatibility issue appears or a maintainer confirms this as a stable release policy.
